# core.autocrlf=true on this machine rewrites checked-out files to CRLF.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-23: `scripts/server/provision.sh` was piped to the host
# over ssh from this worktree and bash answered
#
#     bash: line 22: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# because the line was `set -euo pipefail\r`. Nothing about that message
# points at line endings, and the script had run correctly minutes earlier -
# the CRLF arrived on a later checkout, not from an edit.
#
# Shell scripts and anything executed by a POSIX interpreter keep LF in the
# WORKING TREE, not just in the object store, because the working tree is
# what gets copied, piped and rsync'd to a Linux host.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf

# Same reason: these are read by systemd and by apt on the host.
*.service   text eol=lf
*.timer     text eol=lf

# The sandbox fixture is compared BYTE FOR BYTE against what
# `scripts/sandbox_fixture.py --seed N` regenerates - that is the operator's
# acceptance test for determinism, and
# `test_the_committed_seed_files_match_the_generator` asserts it.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on this machine would check these out with CRLF while
# the generator writes LF, so the comparison would fail on a fresh clone and
# the apparent cause would be the generator rather than the checkout. Same
# failure mode as the shell scripts above, and found the same way: `git add`
# warned "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it".
docs/sandbox/*.json  text eol=lf
